        {
          "id": 2218998067,
          "type": "DiffNote",
          "body": "Yeah, I made the fi->start / max_start comparison \"<=\" below because our infinite or large loops are sometimes the result of overflows in the loop counter which results in the offset cycling through the same values, e.g.\n\n    int offset;\n    while (...) {\n        proto_tree_add_item(...\n        offset += 4;\n        proto_tree_add_item(...\n        offset += 2;\n        proto_tree_add_item(...\n        offset += 2^32 - 6\n    }\n\nThe limit needs to be large enough to skip over legitimate cases of idle offsets but small enough to catch cases like the one above. 100000 seemed like a good start, but I wonder of 10000 or 1000 might be reasonable as well.",
          "author": {
            "id": 860017,
            "username": "geraldcombs",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Gerald Combs",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://gitlab.com/uploads/-/system/user/avatar/860017/avatar.png?v=1788825804",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/geraldcombs"
          },
          "created_at": "2024-11-19T20:03:50.004Z",
          "updated_at": "2024-11-21T16:58:31.780Z",
